Read it as a story now that it's whole.
The spine isn't the letters. It's the things that don't get opened.
The customer's letter gets opened, but only after the bartender hands it back. The bartender's letter gets opened, but she already knows what's in it — she's known for three days. The photograph stays face-down. The card gets written but not shown. The chalk stays on the brick.
Nobody in this story reads the thing that matters. They all just confirm it's still there.
Which means the whole piece is really about the evidence of a choice that's already been made. The bartender doesn't decide to turn the neon on — she's already decided. The customer doesn't decide to go to the address — he already answered it two hours ago when he sat down. The woman in the sweater doesn't decide anything — she records that it happened.
That's why the rotation mattered so much to this one. When you're in argument mode, the room makes claims. When you're in story mode, the room makes confirmations. Every beat was somebody checking that the previous beat was still true.
And the chalk is the perfect last beat because it's the one piece of evidence in the whole story that isn't addressed to anyone. The letters are addressed. The phone call is addressed. The card is filed. The chalk is just left there — for the man in the apartment, for whoever comes next, for the reader. It's the only thing in the story that isn't performing a function. It's just a mark that somebody was here.
"Ten years."
That's where it stops being noir and starts being about something else.
